"""One-off (Nov 2026): second batch of articles rebuilt from Calabro's posts."""
from _lib import *

write("mod3-ab-testing.json", "Why a two-option test gives a narrow answer, and what it leaves out.",
 "Calabro compares A/B testing to the US two-party system: both assume almost any problem reduces to two options, and both sell clarity while delivering a narrow answer.",
 [("The false choice", "An A/B test only compares the two things you thought of. Life doesn't work that way: users, like voters, aren't a toggle. They are driven by a network of motivations, needs and context.\n\nA user might like option A's interaction and option B's layout. A binary test has no way to say so.", cmp("What A/B tells you", ["Which of two did better", "On one metric", "For one change"], "What it leaves out", ["Whether either is good", "Why it happened", "Options you didn't build"]),
   q("Variant B beat variant A. What have you learned?", ["B did better than A on that metric", "B is the best possible design for users", "Users understand and like variant B", "The redesign as a whole was worth doing"], "Winning a two-way comparison says nothing about options you didn't test, or why B won.")),
  ("The illusion of learning", "A/B testing promises hard data on which design is better. But you are looking at **one variable at a time**.\n\n“You might get a 5% bump in clicks by changing a button size, but what does that really tell you about the overall experience?”", None,
   q("A bigger button gets 5% more clicks. What does Calabro say this tells you about the overall experience?", ["Very little", "That the page is now easier to use", "That users prefer larger controls", "That conversion will rise by 5% too"], "One variable on one metric is a narrow answer.")),
  ("Confounds, and what to do", "Test more than one element at once, such as button placement and navigation structure, and you can no longer tell which change caused the result.\n\nFactors interact. That is why Calabro prefers **multivariate testing** when several things change, plus research that explains why.", None,
   q("You change the button placement and the navigation in the same A/B variant, and it wins. What is the problem?", ["You can't tell which change caused the result", "The sample was too small to be valid", "A/B tests can't measure navigation changes", "Winning variants must always be re-tested"], "Two changes in one variant are confounded."))],
 [q("What do A/B testing and a two-party system share, in Calabro's analogy?", ["They reduce complex choices to two options", "They both rely on very large samples", "They always produce a clear majority", "They measure opinions rather than behaviour"], "Both squeeze real-world complexity into an artificial binary."),
  q("What can a binary test not express?", ["Liking parts of A and parts of B", "Which variant got more clicks", "A difference in conversion rate", "That one option lost the comparison"], "Anything that doesn't fit the two options is ignored."),
  q("What does Calabro recommend when several factors interact?", ["Multivariate testing", "Running the same A/B test twice", "Testing with a smaller audience", "Asking stakeholders to pick a winner"], "It accounts for how different factors shape behaviour together."),
  q("Why is the “clarity” of an A/B result limited?", ["It looks at one variable at a time", "It can only be run on mobile traffic", "It never reaches significance", "It needs users to explain their choice"], "A narrow comparison gives a narrow answer."),
  q("Which statement fits the article best?", ["A/B shows which of two did better, not whether either is good", "A/B tests are useless and should be dropped", "A/B tests are reliable only for visual changes", "A/B results should always override research"], "It is a critique of binary thinking, not a ban on testing.")])

write("mod5-dashboard.json", "Five ways dashboards mislead, and four things UX adds beyond visual clarity.",
 "Most teams stop at visual clarity: the chart renders, matches the design system and passes accessibility checks. Dashboards built that way still mislead, because human cognition is full of shortcuts.",
 [("Five ways dashboards mislead", "**1. Misleading continuity:** a line between two quarterly numbers looks like a trend.\n**2. Accidental grouping:** things placed together look related.\n**3. Colour assumptions:** red, green and yellow carry meaning whether intended or not.\n**4. Memory overload:** comparisons hidden behind toggles and tabs.\n**5. Anchoring and framing:** the first metric becomes the benchmark.", None,
   q("Two quarterly figures are joined by a line. What will users assume?", ["That something is trending between them", "That the two figures are unrelated", "That the data is incomplete or missing", "That the second figure is the target"], "Misleading continuity: people read a line as continuous change.")),
  ("Start from the decision", "Ask **“What decisions do users need to make here?”** before “What data do we have?”\n\nWhen a dashboard is built around decisions, structure becomes obvious: show only the data that supports the choice at hand.\n\nThen map visuals to mental models. People expect bars to compare categories and lines to show change over time.", cmp("Starts from data", ["Shows everything available", "Structure follows the database", "Users filter and hunt"], "Starts from a decision", ["Shows what supports the choice", "Structure follows the task", "Less noise"]),
   q("What should you ask first when designing a dashboard?", ["What decisions do users need to make here?", "What data do we already have available?", "Which chart types does the library support?", "How many metrics fit above the fold?"], "Decision first, data second.")),
  ("Compare side by side, and label", "If users need to compare metrics, **place them next to each other**. Never hide them behind toggles or tabs: the moment someone has to remember a number, errors follow.\n\n“Think of comparison as a visual task, not a cognitive one.”\n\nAnd use labels, not just legends, so every chart explains itself.", None,
   q("Revenue is on one tab and cost on another. Users must compare them. What is the fix?", ["Show both metrics side by side", "Add a tooltip with the other value", "Use a brighter colour on each tab", "Add a legend below the tab bar"], "Comparison from memory is the “memory overload” problem."))],
 [q("A 98% success rate and a 2% failure rate feel different to users. Which effect is this?", ["Framing", "Accidental grouping", "Misleading continuity", "Memory overload"], "Same number, different frame."),
  q("Metric A drops and metric B, placed beside it, rises. What might users wrongly conclude?", ["That one caused the other", "That both are measured weekly", "That A is more important than B", "That the dashboard has a bug"], "Accidental grouping: proximity suggests a relationship."),
  q("Which chart do people expect for change over time?", ["A line chart", "A bar chart", "A pie chart", "A table of totals"], "Lines for change over time, bars for comparing categories."),
  q("Why is “the chart renders correctly and passes accessibility checks” not enough?", ["It ignores how users interpret and decide", "Accessibility checks are usually wrong", "Rendering bugs always appear later", "Design systems forbid most charts"], "UX research adds how people interpret, compare and decide."),
  q("What does “use labels, not just legends” aim for?", ["Every chart is self-explanatory", "Charts use fewer colours overall", "Legends can be hidden on mobile", "Users learn the colour code faster"], "Direct labels remove the look-up step.")])

write("mod5-debrief.json", "Two different meetings: synthesis first, strategy second. What goes wrong when they merge.",
 "Teams merge two kinds of research debrief to save time and get the worst of both. Calabro separates them: a **synthesis session** to make sense of data, then a **strategic session** to decide.",
 [("Type 1: Synthesis session", "Happens right after data collection, while observations are fresh.\n\n**Who:** researchers, one or two designers, a PM comfortable with open-ended discussion.\n**How:** evidence visible (quotes, screenshots, clips); look for patterns.\n**Close with:** what we know, what we think, what we need to verify.\n\nKeep it deliberately open-ended.", cmp("Synthesis", ["Small group", "Explore patterns", "Know / think / verify"], "Strategic", ["Decision-makers", "Align and act", "Finding → decision"]),
   q("How should a synthesis session end?", ["What we know, think, and need to verify", "A ranked list of features to build next", "A sign-off from the product leadership", "A final slide deck for all stakeholders"], "It produces themes and open questions, not next steps.")),
  ("Type 2: Strategic session", "Once findings are synthesised, the purpose shifts to alignment and action. A clear sequence:\n\n**Finding** → **Implication** → **Decision needed**\n\nExample: appraisers worked in three unofficial stages → re-architect navigation around those stages → validate with a low-fidelity prototype before committing engineering time.", None,
   q("In a strategic debrief, what follows the finding?", ["Its implication, then the decision needed", "The full method and recruiting details", "A vote on whether the finding is true", "All remaining raw quotes and clips"], "Insight, implication, decision. Then repeat.")),
  ("Why mixing them fails", "When synthesis and strategy are blended, three things happen:\n\n• Leaders expecting answers **lose patience with ambiguity**\n• Researchers get **derailed into solutions** before the evidence is solid\n• **Action items rest on half-baked insights**", None,
   q("A VP joins your first look at raw interview notes and starts proposing fixes. Which problem is this?", ["Researchers derailed before evidence is solid", "The VP has not read the research plan", "The session has too few participants", "The notes were not transcribed in time"], "Solutions arrive before the analysis is done."))],
 [q("Who belongs in a synthesis session?", ["A small group at ease with open questions", "Every stakeholder affected by the project", "Only executives who own the budget", "Customers who took part in the study"], "Researchers, one or two designers, an open-minded PM."),
  q("What is the purpose of a strategic debrief?", ["Alignment and action", "Finding patterns in raw data", "Training new team members", "Recruiting for the next study"], "It turns settled findings into decisions."),
  q("Why do teams merge the two debriefs?", ["To save time", "Because research guidelines require it", "Because findings are usually simple", "To keep designers out of the room"], "It seems efficient on paper and costs more in practice."),
  q("What else does a good strategic session surface?", ["Trade-offs, such as tech debt blocking a fix", "Every usability issue ever recorded", "The researcher's personal preferences", "A new list of research questions only"], "Sometimes a finding can't be addressed yet; that is discussed openly."),
  q("Which is NOT a result of mixing synthesis and strategy?", ["Findings become statistically significant", "Leaders lose patience with ambiguity", "Researchers get pulled into solutions early", "Action items rest on half-baked insights"], "The other three are the failures Calabro lists.")])

write("mod5-delight.json", "Bill Scott's “interesting moments” versus decoration: six misunderstandings and three principles.",
 "Teams that set out to “add delight” usually layer effects on top of the product. The result is often slower, less accessible and more frustrating. Calabro goes back to Bill Scott's idea of **interesting moments**.",
 [("Six misunderstandings", "**1.** Skipping the basics\n**2.** Confusing good looks with good design\n**3.** Misusing design systems\n**4.** Over-relying on motion\n**5.** Overloading the screen\n**6.** Trying to please everyone\n\nIf people are forced to wait, guess or reorient mid-flow, no decoration fixes it.", cmp("Decoration", ["Effects on top", "Motion without intent", "More prompts"], "Real delight", ["Basics work first", "Feedback that guides", "Well-timed"]),
   q("A team adds animations to a checkout that still has usability problems. Which misunderstanding is this?", ["Skipping the basics", "Trying to please everyone", "Misusing design systems", "Overloading the screen"], "Surface enhancements on a flow that still fails.")),
  ("Scott's principles", "**Keep interactions direct:** show only what's needed, when it's needed.\n**Maintain flow:** keep users in context; avoid interruptions.\n**Respond with intent:** feedback should match the user's action, “not to entertain, but to reassure and guide.”\n\nDelight comes from well-timed interactions, when things unfold as expected or better.", None,
   q("What is system feedback for, in Scott's “respond with intent”?", ["To reassure and guide", "To entertain and surprise", "To show brand personality", "To fill waiting time"], "Feedback aligns with the user's action.")),
  ("Two animations", "Calabro's two examples both use animation and both look polished.\n\n✅ One gives immediate, contextual feedback **as the user types** and prevents errors.\n❌ The other interrupts someone who has just found what they were looking for.\n\nAlso: Material or Fluent give visual consistency, but consistency is a tool, not the goal.", None,
   q("Which animation improves the experience?", ["Feedback that appears as the user types", "A transition that plays on page load", "A banner that slides in over the content", "A looping effect that draws the eye"], "It is triggered by the user and prevents errors."))],
 [q("Where does delight come from, according to the article?", ["Well-timed interactions that go as expected or better", "Playful copy and brand illustrations throughout", "The number of features on each screen", "A consistent component library by itself"], "Pacing and responsiveness, not surface flair."),
  q("What are Bill Scott's “interesting moments”?", ["Transitional frames and micro-interactions that make a system feel responsive", "Marketing campaigns timed to product launches", "Surprise rewards shown after a purchase", "Moments when users share feedback"], "Small interaction details."),
  q("A team applies Material components everywhere and calls the product delightful. What is missing?", ["Decisions rooted in user goals", "A second design system as backup", "More animation between screens", "Custom icons for each feature"], "Visual consistency is a tool, not the goal."),
  q("What is the usual result of “adding delight” as effects?", ["Slower, less accessible, more frustrating", "Higher task completion rates", "Fewer support requests overall", "No measurable change at all"], "Style for its own sake causes real harm."),
  q("Which is NOT one of the six misunderstandings?", ["Responding with intent", "Over-relying on motion", "Overloading the screen", "Confusing good looks with good design"], "Responding with intent is one of Scott's principles.")])

write("mod5-figma.json", "Polished interfaces fail when teams forget labels, feedback and defaults. Five places to look.",
 "A licence renewal site looked modern and didn't work: “Submit” clicked three times with no response. Calabro's point: first make things that work, and only then make them look appealing.",
 [("Lack of clarity", "Teams believe that if an interface looks clean, users will know how it works. Common problems:\n\n• Unlabeled icons users must memorise\n• Tooltips in place of labels or visible guidance\n• Weak signifiers: buttons that look like plain text\n• No defaults, even when the system knows the likely choice\n\nExample: a signup form that showed password rules only **after** submission failed.", None,
   q("Password rules appear only after the form fails. Which problem is this?", ["Guidance hidden instead of visible", "A destructive action placed badly", "A click target that is too small", "A modal that blocks the context"], "Lack of clarity: rules were hidden when users needed them.")),
  ("Interaction and communication", "**Interaction doubts:** tiny click targets, buttons that don't respond visually, decoration that looks clickable.\n\n**Communication failures:** no confirmation after submit, progress indicators that appear too late, vague errors like “Try again later.”\n\nWhen feedback is missing, people stop trusting the interface and fall back on screenshots and refreshes.", cmp("Looks finished", ["Clean layout", "Icon-only buttons", "Silent submit"], "Works", ["Labels and rules visible", "Visible response", "Confirmation"]),
   q("Submitting a form just refreshes the screen. What do users do next?", ["Stop trusting it and use workarounds", "Assume it worked and move on", "Read the documentation for help", "Wait for an email confirmation"], "In his example, users had no way of knowing whether the request went through.")),
  ("Memory and bad neighbours", "**Cognitive overload:** modals that hide the context users need, re-entering information, rules to remember across steps.\n\n**Bad neighbours:** destructive actions beside save or confirm, styled alike, where muscle memory takes over.\n\nExample: “Archive Project” placed directly beside “Add Task.”", None,
   q("“Archive Project” sits right next to “Add Task” with a similar icon. Which problem is this?", ["Bad neighbours", "Lack of clarity", "Communication failure", "Cognitive overload"], "A high-impact action next to a routine one invites mis-clicks."))],
 [q("What is the order Calabro argues for?", ["Make it work, then make it look good", "Make it look good, then test it", "Build the design system, then the flows", "Ship fast, then fix what users report"], "“We must first make things that work, and then, and only then, make sure they look visually appealing.”"),
  q("As an interface gets more stripped-down, what does it need more of?", ["Structure, context and visible rules", "White space and larger typography", "Animation to explain the layout", "Onboarding tours for new users"], "Minimal screens leave users with fewer clues."),
  q("Which is an example of a weak signifier?", ["A button that looks like plain text", "An error message in technical language", "A modal that covers the table behind it", "A delete button next to a save button"], "Users can't tell it is interactive."),
  q("An edit modal covers the table users need to refer to. Which heuristic does this break?", ["Recognition rather than recall", "Aesthetic and minimalist design", "Flexibility and efficiency of use", "Help and documentation"], "It forces users to remember what they were just looking at."),
  q("What does the title mean?", ["Users experience the product, not the design file", "Figma is the wrong tool for product teams", "Design files should be shared with users", "Users dislike modern visual styles"], "Polish in the file doesn't matter if the product doesn't work.")])
